# Every app must look like itself

The factory's differentiator is design, not code. A correct app that looks like an unstyled
Material demo is a failed recipe even when every gate is green: nothing about it is worth
installing, and nothing about it is worth paying for. The owner's instruction, recorded
2026-09-26 while reviewing `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook`: an app must be unique and
beautiful by design, not "just native buttons from the standard library" — every app gets its
own pronounced style.

The record: `apps/2026-09-25-pipe-sizing` shipped a working app whose store pack was rendered by
the generic renderer, with an icon and screenshots that had nothing to do with the product. The
studio (`store/studio.json` → `scripts/store-studio.py`) and this rule exist because that is the
failure mode: the factory has to make an app's visual identity a **build input**, not a thing that
gets decorated on at the end if time allows. `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook` is the reference
implementation — deep indigo with amber reserved for work that needs attention, a slot-owned
mark, hero panels with real stats, a section strip, and an icon that reads at 48dp.

## The bar

A slot is not styled until all of these are true.

- **The identity is declared before the UI is styled.** `store/studio.json` names the `mark`, the
  full `palette` (every role the renderer draws with), the per-shot `headlines` and `kickers`, the
  `featureTagline` and the `featurePill`. It is not optional and it is not documentation: it is what
  the launcher icon, the adaptive-icon vectors, the feature graphic and the store compositions are
  rendered from, and `store-kit.py` refuses to render rather than silently falling back to the
  generic pack. Decide the identity at stage 1, not during `/release`.
- **The palette is one source of truth, shared with the app.** The colours in `studio.json` are the
  colours in the slot's `ui/theme/Color.kt`. A store pack whose palette drifted from the app's own
  theme is a screenshot of a different app; `scripts/visual-qa.py`'s `palette` section measures the
  shipped pixels against the Kotlin palette to catch exactly that.
- **The slot owns its theme**: its own `Color.kt`, its own shapes and typography, its own dark and
  light mapping. The Material 3 defaults — including the default purple seed and dynamic colour —
  are a starting point to replace, not a finished look.
- **The slot owns components.** At least the shared vocabulary of the app is slot code, not library
  calls: a mark glyph, a hero/stat panel, a section strip or tabs that are the slot's own, stat
  tiles, panels, empty states, a status pill. `ui/components/Brand.kt` in the reference slot is the
  shape to imitate; its values are not. A component earns its place by encoding a decision the app
  makes repeatedly (a grade, an average with its working, a register state), not by wrapping `Card`.
- **Depth and hierarchy are visible**: a gradient or tonal field, elevation that means something,
  a glyph set used as iconography, spacing that separates groups. Not a grid of identical cards on
  a flat background.
- **The identity survives translation.** The strip in the reference slot renders an inactive pill as
  its mark alone because seven labelled pills cannot fit once the longest locale's words are in
  them. Any layout whose style depends on English string lengths (a fixed-width pill, a tab row of
  labels, a one-line headline) is a defect that only appears after localisation.
- **The icon is drawn, not borrowed.** It is generated from `studio.json`'s `mark`, it reads at
  48dp, it survives the adaptive-icon masks (72dp circle, squircle, 66dp safe zone), and it carries
  no baked-in rounded mask or shadow — Play rejects those, and `visual-qa.py`'s `icon` section
  measures them.

## What is banned

- A screen whose entire visual language is library components with default styling — a stock
  `TopAppBar` plus a stock `TabRow` plus the default `Button` and `OutlinedTextField`, arranged in
  `Card`s on the default background. That is the "just tabs and buttons and numbers" app.
- Shipping the first palette you thought of, or the same palette in two slots.
- Emoji, clipart, or a stock icon as the app's mark or as UI iconography.
- A styled store pack over an unstyled app. The style must be in the build the user installs, and
  `media-capture.mjs` photographs real screens with asserted readings — a mocked-up pretty frame
  around a plain screen is a false claim, not a marketing choice.
- Deferring the style to `/release`. By then the layout and the components are fixed, and restyling
  is a rewrite of every screen instead of a decision made once.

## Identity is per slot, like the job is

Two slots must not share a mark, a palette, or a component kit for the same reason they must not
share a job: Google Play's Repetitive Content policy treats near-duplicate apps as a terminating
offence, and a catalogue of apps that look identical is the same evidence as a catalogue of apps
that do the same thing — see `docs/monetization-strategy.md` §1.2. "Our house style" is a name for
reusing a slot's identity. Derive a new one per slot from that slot's own subject.

## How it is verified

- `store/studio.json` is a declaration: `scripts/verify-slot.mjs` treats a pack that declares it as
  composed and requires `store/provenance.json` to bind the committed assets to a recorded build,
  so `rm store/studio.json store/provenance.json` cannot drop the check.
- `python scripts/visual-qa.py --slot <slug>` measures the shipped pixels independently: palette
  against the app's own Kotlin palette, caption ink and edge clipping, WCAG contrast of caption and
  mark, pairwise similarity of the six phone shots (six near-identical frames is not a pack), the
  adaptive-mask clipping of the icon, and legibility of the caption at 360/180/90 px wide.
- `node scripts/media-capture.mjs --slot <slug>` is what makes the claim true: it replays the
  journey against the signed release build and refuses a frame that does not show the readings its
  shot declares.

None of these can say the app is beautiful. They can say the app's declared identity is the one in
the build, the one in the pack, and the one in the pixels — and they can say two slots do not have
the same one. `visual-qa.py` is a **report, not a gate**: it prints per-asset PASS/FAIL rows for
Play's own asset rules and a `stability` verdict, but it exits 0 whenever it finishes, so a clean
run is evidence to read, never an approval — the objective gates are `scripts/verify-slot.mjs`, and
the judgement that separates a distinctive style from an ugly one stays with the owner, at the
phase-1 review and the phase-2 approval; this rule's job is to ensure there is something to judge.
